  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Trevor McDonald lasted 2.1 innings. Toronto scored eight runs in two innings and the game was over by the third.",
    "what_this_means": "Toronto arrived with a clear plan. Get after the early starter, stack runs before the bullpen took over, and let the margin do the work. It worked in brutal fashion. McDonald faced eleven hitters, gave up eight runs, and left with the game already decided. The bullpen. Adrian Houser for 5.2 innings, Ryan Walker for the final inning. Was clean and sharp in relief, but there was nothing to protect. The offense scraped together nine hits and two runners batted in. Luis Arraez went 2-for-4 with an RBI, Heliot Ramos added 2-for-4, and the rest logged six strikeouts in a game that stopped mattering before the fourth inning started. This is what inconsistency looks like in real time: a lineup that can string hits together but cannot string runs together, a starter who gets shelled and never comes back, and a blowout that was decided before half the stadium paid attention.",
    "what_to_watch": "Dylan Cease takes the mound tonight for Toronto with a 2.79 ERA. Logan Webb comes back after posting a 3.12 ERA over his last four starts, searching for the kind of outing that keeps these games close enough to be won.",
